  "worked_solution": "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{2}^{bx} \\\\  & ={2}^{b(2)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) \\\\ {2}^{2b} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2b} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ 2b & =3 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
